{
  "id": "80784",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "The Reaction Quotient",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A chemical equation gives a rule for how molecules react with one another. However, it does not describe a single state of a chemical system. Instead, the reaction leads to a range of possible states that we can view as lying along a coordinate that we will call the progress of reaction. Let's revisit the reversible reaction for the reactants hydrogen and iodine and the product hydrogen iodide:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "m1kgtkb2otb7y06",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "zq5gr3gry91l4wn",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "aq1tft6258mv3vc",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm H}_2(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm I}_2(g)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;2\\;\\mathrm{HI}(g)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "If this reaction begins with only reactants, for example, five molecules of H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " and five molecules of I"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ", those molecules will begin to react to form HI. If this reaction goes to completion, all of the molecules of H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " and I"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " will be converted to HI, and once the reaction is complete, there will be 10 molecules of HI and 0 molecules of H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " and I"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ". The diagram below represents the progress of this reaction. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "dpw87zsndfku4jw",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "In the forward reaction, from left to right, the H"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " and I"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " combine to form HI. In the reverse reaction, from right to left, the HI regenerates H"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " and I"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ".   "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3861021590",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "039g6or955jtfi8",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "drs5d1q4861u34w",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/14/14e437337b11ae4eb1607020f9dc8c12/progress_hi_reaction.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "700"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "As we move from left to right, the reactants (in this case, H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " and I"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ") combine to form the products (in this case, HI). As we move from right to left, the product (HI) regenerates the reactants (H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " and I"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "). At the far right, the forward reaction has progressed until the reactants have run out and the reaction is completed."
              }
            ],
            "id": "yte32u3d3oa3r84",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "However, this is a reversible reaction, so as soon as some of the product (HI) forms, the reverse reaction (making H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " and I"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ") starts to occur. When the rate of the reverse reaction (HI forming H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " and I"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ") equals the rate of the forward reaction (H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " and I"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " forming HI), the reaction reaches equilibrium. The equilibrium state occurs between the end points, and the equilibrium mixture contains both reactants and products. The diagram below represents the progress of this reaction until it reaches equilibrium at a particular temperature. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "2sjbi4alyq7s170",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "lwbyqaez2vpb5eg",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "0b0mu4yh3w8l1uy",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/bc/bcceeb3209252e3618118c700e7496aa/progress_hi_rxn_equil.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "700"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "As this reaction occurs, the amounts of H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " and I"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " decrease and the amount of HI increases. At some point in the progress of the reaction ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "t"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": " in this case), the concentrations of all substances are constant, and the reaction has reached equilibrium."
              }
            ],
            "id": "w691kgkjgwqsymo",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "At any point in its progression, the status of a reversible reaction (the relative amounts of reactants and products) can be expressed mathematically by evaluating the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "reaction quotient"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A mathematical function that compares the concentrations or partial pressures of products to reactants at any point during a reaction, calculated using the same expression as the equilibrium constant ("
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "K"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": ") but for conditions that are not necessarily at equilibrium."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2554304633",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4022685197",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". "
              }
            ],
            "id": "e0x3oz17vydvqm1",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "uwkti5e35g8wahd",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "tvskifmbh85atd6",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/A3/A31F9808B584CB71B1FEA56DD0BCDE4E/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "2wbyx9ge6s5ehvc",
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "23moejz2ym1nlrs",
            "items": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "59366693",
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "For the general reversible reaction:"
                      }
                    ],
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " ",
                        "type": "text"
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                    "src": "a\\mathrm A\\;+\\;b\\mathrm B\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;c\\mathrm C\\;+\\;d\\mathrm D",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "the reaction quotient "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "Q"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "c"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " is derived directly from the stoichiometry of the balanced equation as:"
                      }
                    ],
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " ",
                        "type": "text"
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                    "src": "Q_\\mathrm{c}\\;=\\;\\frac{\\lbrack\\mathrm C\\rbrack^c\\lbrack\\mathrm D\\rbrack^d}{\\lbrack\\mathrm A\\rbrack^a\\lbrack\\mathrm B\\rbrack^b}",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "where the subscript indicates the use of concentrations in molarity units. This reaction quotient equation is a simplification of the more rigorous expression that uses relative values of concentration that are unitless, so "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "Q"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "c"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " is unitless as well. We use concentrations in units of molarity and disregard units when calculating "
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "Q"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "c"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2817652033",
                    "type": "p"
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": " "
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3299428502",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "dd"
              }
            ],
            "title": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "term": true,
                    "text": "The Reaction Quotient, "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "term": true,
                    "text": "Q"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "82437782",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "type": "dl"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "bbe6dnqurm5m0l9",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "p8qcq8i51d2tioz",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/A3/A31F9808B584CB71B1FEA56DD0BCDE4E/green_line.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "2463229954",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "119630036",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          "focus": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              1,
              0
            ]
          }
        },
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "For the reversible reaction:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "uk4vw670lhq0duw",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "qj45cr9lj5ndz5v",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "8urzceyt348s7cd",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "{\\mathrm H}_2(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm I}_2(g)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;2\\;\\mathrm{HI}(g)",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "write the reaction quotient expression, then determine the values of "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Q"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "c"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " at each time shown in the progress of reaction diagram above ("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "t"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "0"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " through "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "t"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "5"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "). Each molecule in the diagram represents 1.0 M of that substance."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "nre15vouchxug1r",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The reaction quotient expression is written with products in the numerator, reactants in the denominator, and each substance's concentration raised to the power that equals its stoichiometric coefficient:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "x1efkomsnsbmf1n",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "tiff2uagnu5kmwn",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "lyk9bbt2bz61tlq",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "Q_\\mathrm{c}\\;=\\;\\frac{\\lbrack\\mathrm{HI}\\rbrack^2}{\\lbrack{\\mathrm H}_2\\rbrack\\lbrack{\\mathrm I}_2\\rbrack}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "This equation can be used to evaluate "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Q"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "c"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " for each time in the progression of the reaction. For example, at time "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "t"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "1"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", there are four H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " molecules and four I"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " molecules, so [H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "] = [I"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "] = 4.0 M. There are two HI molecules, so [HI] = 2.0 M. Then, substituting these values into the "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Q"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "c"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " expression:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "ov2jut6gzwha1df",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "g9d0jf6hh8n7y4l",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "b5nippbyg3q5nhi",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "Q_\\mathrm{c}\\;=\\;\\frac{\\lbrack\\mathrm{HI}\\rbrack^2}{\\lbrack{\\mathrm H}_2\\rbrack\\lbrack{\\mathrm I}_2\\rbrack}\\; =\\;\\frac{\\left(2.0\\right)^2}{\\left(4.0\\right)\\left(4.0\\right)}\\;=\\;0.25",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Similar calculations can be done to determine "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Q"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "c"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " at the other times. The results are shown below."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "ek5vej9n8p7bxq9",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "b8z3kckw5hc4dl0",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "8xcirpb4ser1vfl",
                "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/be/be57112aef6fe30b6ba39042390dfc08/q_calc_hi_rxn.png",
                "type": "img",
                "width": "700"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Starting with only reactants, the reactant concentrations decrease, and the product concentration increases, so the reaction quotient increases until equilibrium is reached and the concentrations of all substances are constant."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "eyj0k71amywdt5i",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Because "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Q"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is a ratio of the product concentrations to the reactant concentrations, it can provide a rough estimate of the relative amounts of reactants and products at any point in the progress of the reaction. For the simplest reactions with 1:1 stoichiometry, if "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Q"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is smaller than 1, there are more reactants than products, and if it is larger than 1, there are more products than reactants. For more complicated reaction stoichiometry, this is not always the case. However, in general, if "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Q"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is much larger than 1, there are more products than reactants, and if "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Q"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is much smaller than 1, there are more reactants than products."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "lqwrczxifb60x1n",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "425567081",
            "selection": {
              "anchor": {
                "offset": 0,
